Create a new microfrontend named "Code Intelligence" within the existing application.

Before implementation:
- Analyze the existing project structure, architecture, and microfrontend organization.
- Strictly reuse the existing application template, layout, theme, navigation, components, and coding conventions.
- Reuse existing shared packages whenever possible.
- Create or install additional packages only when necessary.
- Integrate the new microfrontend into the existing routing and navigation.
- Do not modify unrelated application features.

Technology:
- React 19
- TypeScript
- Mermaid.js for interactive graph visualization
- Monaco Editor for Java source code
- Existing API client and state management solutions

The microfrontend must communicate with the existing Java Spring Boot backend.

Features:

1. Project Explorer
   - Browse modules, packages, classes, interfaces, and methods.
   - Search and filter elements.
   - Display class and method metadata.

2. Interactive Graph Explorer
   - Visualize method calls and dependencies using Mermaid.
   - Support zoom, pan, node selection, and dynamic expansion.
   - Show callers and callees.
   - Navigate between classes and methods.
   - Display dependency paths.
   - Support module, package, class, and method graph views.

3. Source Code Viewer
   - Display Java source code using Monaco Editor.
   - Clicking a method in Mermaid opens its source code.
   - Highlight the selected method.
   - Navigate between method calls and definitions.
   - Display method parameters, return types, and metadata.

4. Architecture Analysis
   - Display physical and logical modules.
   - Visualize dependencies and cycles.
   - Display class relevance, coupling, and impact metrics.

5. User Interface
   - Use the existing application design system.
   - Provide three resizable panels:
     Left: Project Explorer
     Center: Mermaid Graph
     Right: Monaco Source Editor
   - Support existing light/dark themes.
   - Add contextual menus and navigation controls.
   - Preserve selection and navigation history.

Performance:
- The analyzed project contains approximately 1,790 classes and 22,000 methods.
- Load data progressively.
- Use lazy graph expansion.
- Avoid rendering the entire graph.
- Cache API responses using existing project conventions.
- Keep Mermaid rendering responsive.

Architecture:
- Implement this feature as an independent microfrontend.
- Respect existing microfrontend boundaries and integration mechanisms.
- Reuse shared UI, API, configuration, and utility packages.
- Keep graph visualization components reusable.
- Use actual backend REST APIs.
- Do not create mock data or fake endpoints.
- Do not modify the backend.

Mandatory rules:
- No code comments.
- No test files or test classes.
- No documentation files.
- No unnecessary dependencies.
- No placeholder implementations.
- Respect the existing application template and conventions.
- Do not recreate components already available in shared packages.
- Do not introduce a new design system.
- Do not modify unrelated microfrontends.

Implement autonomously without unnecessary confirmation requests.

Build the microfrontend, resolve compilation errors, and ensure it integrates correctly with the existing application.
